--TEST--
`Core\Queue\Id::parse` and `tryParse` take a `tainted` string, and the Id's own string is a plain `string` — `rule:security/tainted-qualifier`, `rule:expressions/try-parse`
--FILE--
<?nvs
use Core\Queue\Id;

// A job id arrives from a client, so it is `tainted`. Both members accept it.
// Neither one opens a connection, so this case needs no queue.
tainted string $fromClient = "12.ABEiM0RVZneImaq7zN3u_w.cmVjZWlwdHM";
Id $id = Core\Queue\Id::parse($fromClient);

// The Id has no text a program can read except `toString`, which is built only
// from checked parts. So it is a plain `string`.
string $plain = $id->toString();
echo $plain, "\n";

// A string that is not an Id gives `null` from `tryParse` and an error from `parse`.
tainted string $wrong = "12.not-a-token.cmVjZWlwdHM";
echo "tried ", Core\Queue\Id::tryParse($wrong) == null ? "null" : "an id", "\n";
try {
    Core\Queue\Id::parse($wrong);
    echo "parsed\n";
} catch (RuntimeError $refused) {
    echo "refused: ", $refused->message, "\n";
}
--EXPECT--
12.ABEiM0RVZneImaq7zN3u_w.cmVjZWlwdHM
tried null
refused: Core\Queue\Id::parse(): the string is not a job id, because its second part is not a job token. A job id is the string that `toString` writes
